Subject: Phrasing results for multi-species occupancy model
Message-ID: <qIUNjXNFpFI.3IFdQSZ2AAAJ@hmecology.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/hmecology/c/qIUNjXNFpFI/m/3IFdQSZ2AAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello all,

I have a
question about phrasing when reporting results for a multi-species occupancy
model. Apologies if this is pedantic, but I want to make sure I’m presenting
things correctly.

I’ve
modelled the data such that I have species-specific occupancy probabilities for
each habitat type in my study area, and I’m then comparing the occupancy values
between the habitat types. I’ve defined occupancy to be “the probability
of a species using the area sampled by the camera trap during the survey
period.”

I’ve been
writing the results something like “Species-specific occupancy probability was
higher for gibbons in Kerangas than in Low Pole.” But someone else wants to
write is as “Gibbons were more likely to occupy Kerangas than Low Pole”,
and I’m getting hung up on the “more likely to occupy”. I think I’m interpreting
that phrase as “more likely to exist in Habitat X” which doesn’t feel quite
right given the definition of occupancy probability. I also haven’t come across
any papers that phrase the results in that way, so I’m unsure if it’s appropriate.

I may be
overthinking this and am just confusing myself, but if anyone has thoughts on phrasing it that way, that
would be appreciated!

Many thanks,
Shari

From u00ebeb6e0521112d at groups.google.com  Thu Oct 13 10:07:46 2022
From: u00ebeb6e0521112d at groups.google.com (Marc Kery)
